-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Collapsed Node Access**: If a `collapse()` operation previously set `children = null` on an internal node (at `depth < maxDepth`), a subsequent `set()` will throw an error when accessing `node.children[childIdx]` [S1, unofficial]. The implementation must check `node.isLeaf` or `!node.children` before descending and expand the node if necessary [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Null Color Removal**: Using `set(x, y, z, null)` to remove voxels leaves leaf nodes with `color = null`, which prevents effective pruning/collapsing and can permanently "materialize" empty regions [S1, unofficial]. A dedicated `delete(x, y, z)` function is recommended to remove nodes and prune upward when a branch becomes entirely empty [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Out of Bounds**: If the voxel position is outside the current node's bounding box, the function should return early to avoid infinite recursion or invalid memory access [S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Collapsed Node Access:** If a `collapse()` operation previously set `children = null` on an internal node at `depth < maxDepth`, a subsequent `set()` will throw an error when accessing `node.children[childIdx]` because `node.children` is null [S1, unofficial]. The implementation must check `node.isLeaf` (or `!node.children`) before descending and expand the node into 8 children initialized as leaves with the prior uniform color [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Deletion via Null Color:** Using `set(x, y, z, null)` to remove voxels leaves leaf nodes with `color = null`, which prevents effective pruning/collapsing and can permanently "materialize" empty regions [S1, unofficial]. A dedicated `delete(x, y, z)` function is required to remove nodes and prune upward when a branch becomes entirely empty [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Octree-based Mesh Voxelization
-*   **What it is**: A method that converts a triangle mesh into a voxel grid using an octree structure.
-*   **Difference**: Focuses on rasterizing geometry into voxels rather than inserting individual voxels into a sparse structure.
-*   **When to choose**: When starting from a mesh model.
-*   **Link**: [Octree-based Mesh Voxelization](3071-octree-based-mesh-voxelization.md)
+### [OpenVDB Sparse Tree Allocation on Voxel Activation](10331-openvdb-sparse-tree-allocation-on-voxel-activation.md)
+This method describes the memory allocation and node initialization behavior of the OpenVDB sparse tree when a new voxel is activated at a specific coordinate [10331-openvdb-sparse-tree-allocation-on-voxel-activation.md]. It solves the problem of understanding why activating a single sparse voxel can result in a disproportionately large memory footprint due to the bulk initialization of sibling tiles along the path to the leaf [10331-openvdb-sparse-tree-allocation-on-voxel-activation.md]. It is distinct from standard dense voxelization because it allocates full tile structures for inactive siblings at every level of the octree hierarchy, rather than leaving them as null pointers or compact bitfields [10331-openvdb-sparse-tree-allocation-on-voxel-activation.md].
 
-### Dynamic Sparse Voxel Octree with Morton Codes
-*   **What it is**: An SVO implementation that uses Morton codes (Z-order curves) for efficient spatial indexing and traversal.
-*   **Difference**: Uses linearized indices for memory layout and traversal, whereas this method uses explicit tree pointers.
-*   **When to choose**: When memory locality and cache efficiency are critical, or for out-of-core storage.
-*   **Link**: [Dynamic Sparse Voxel Octree with Morton Codes](4099-dynamic-sparse-voxel-octree-with-morton-codes.md)
+### Voxel Octree Construction from Edge Data
+This variant constructs voxels in three steps for the X-, Y-, and Z-axes based on edge data [S3]. It decomposes the edge's starting grid point index `i` into local grid coordinates `x, y, z` using grid resolution `n` [S3]. The coordinates are calculated as:
+$$x = i \pmod{(n+1)}$$
+$$y = \lfloor (i \pmod{n+1})^2 \rfloor \div (n+1) \rfloor$$
+$$z = \lfloor i \div (n+1)^2 \rfloor$$
+(Note: The source contains a likely typo in the y-formula regarding the square and floor placement, but quotes the source exactly) [S3]. After translating coordinates to world positions, the method adds offsets to identify voxel positions and retrieves or creates voxels if they exist within the grid bounds [S3]. A pitfall is that if adjusted coordinates lie outside the bounds of the material grid, no voxel exists for that offset [S3].
 
