-### When to Use
-*   Not given in the sources.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(D)$ where $D$ is the depth of the octree, as the algorithm traverses one path from root to leaf.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Depends on the number of active voxels and the depth. In the worst case (fully expanded tree), it is proportional to the number of nodes in the path. The expansion of collapsed nodes can temporarily increase memory usage [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple recursive structure; handles dynamic insertion and deletion (with proper delete implementation); supports variable density via sparse representation.
+*   **Limitations:** Recursive nature may cause stack overflow for very deep trees if not implemented iteratively; cache performance can be poor due to pointer chasing (mitigated by Morton codes in siblings [S2, unofficial]); expansion of collapsed nodes can be costly if done frequently [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When dynamic modification of the voxel grid is required (e.g., sculpting, editing) and the data is sparse [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When the grid is dense and static, or when cache locality is critical without additional linearization techniques [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-
-### Origin
-The method is derived from implementations of Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO) for real-time rendering and voxel editing [S1, S2, unofficial]. The specific logic for handling collapse/expand states and the pitfalls of null-color removal are documented in community-driven development projects [S1, S2, unofficial].
-
-### History
-SVOs were popularized for ray marching in real-time graphics [G]. The specific "placeVoxel" implementation with explicit collapse handling appears in modern web-based voxel editors and tools [S1, S2, unofficial].
-
-### Usage
-Used in voxel-based games, CAD tools, and scientific visualization where dynamic modification of volumetric data is required [S1, S2, unofficial].
+The method originates from implementations of Sparse Voxel Octrees (SVO) for real-time rendering and voxel editing. The specific pitfalls regarding collapsed nodes and null-color deletion were highlighted in community discussions on GitHub regarding the Voxelarchitect project [S1, unofficial]. The recursive structure is a standard approach in octree manipulation, adapted here for voxel color/value storage [S1, S2, unofficial]. The variant using edge data for construction is described in research on volumetric terrain rendering [S3].
 
